1
00:00:02,977 --> 00:00:19,186
[SPEAKER_01]: I was speaking to a friend recently about seclusion, and what I was saying was that seclusion is neither good nor bad, but it's really what you do inside seclusion that makes it, makes it so.

2
00:00:21,747 --> 00:00:30,572
[SPEAKER_01]: And we're speaking about more of a... if your mind is defiled, then it's not... seclusion is not going to make it better.

3
00:00:35,518 --> 00:00:36,499
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what we said before.

4
00:00:37,479 --> 00:00:40,281
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion is like an amplifier.

5
00:00:40,841 --> 00:00:44,683
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it will amplify whatever you carry, whatever you have.

6
00:00:45,903 --> 00:00:48,965
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you're trying to determine whether seclusion is good or bad.

7
00:00:49,565 --> 00:00:51,967
[SPEAKER_00]: It's trying to determine whether amplification is good or bad.

8
00:00:52,707 --> 00:00:54,028
[SPEAKER_00]: But in itself, that's neutral.

9
00:00:55,388 --> 00:01:00,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Amplification is neither good nor bad, but things that you amplify will determine whether it's good or bad.

10
00:01:00,471 --> 00:01:01,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you're amplifying

11
00:01:02,657 --> 00:01:07,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Bad states of mind, or are you amplifying the wholesome ones, the good ones?

12
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:09,321
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seclusion, that's all, that's all it is, really.

13
00:01:10,722 --> 00:01:12,804
[SPEAKER_00]: It will only increase what you already have.

14
00:01:12,924 --> 00:01:16,406
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha himself said the person needs to be ready first.

15
00:01:18,227 --> 00:01:26,492
[SPEAKER_00]: Restrained by the rules of patimokkha, develop mind in samādhi, develop the right view, before that person can benefit from solitude.

16
00:01:27,533 --> 00:01:30,095
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't mean you shouldn't be trying to develop solitude.

17
00:01:31,052 --> 00:01:35,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's another way to find out whether you are carrying good or bad, mostly.

18
00:01:35,753 --> 00:01:35,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

19
00:01:36,013 --> 00:01:38,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Put yourself in seclusion and you see what gets amplified.

20
00:01:39,874 --> 00:01:42,194
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, okay, now the bad states are amplified, which...

21
00:01:42,414 --> 00:01:43,415
[SPEAKER_00]: I must address that.

22
00:01:44,055 --> 00:01:49,036
[SPEAKER_00]: I mustn't develop a view, oh, I will never go to seclusion again, because it amplified all these bad things.

23
00:01:49,136 --> 00:01:50,856
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it amplified things that you need to do.

24
00:01:51,596 --> 00:01:53,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from that point of view, seclusion is always good.

25
00:01:54,837 --> 00:01:59,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Good as an indicator of what work needs to be done and to what extent.

26
00:01:59,833 --> 00:02:07,938
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sometimes you don't notice things, you go into seclusion and it will amplify whatever is really there, fundamentally underlying your moods and so on.

27
00:02:08,078 --> 00:02:09,679
[SPEAKER_00]: And once it's amplified, you know it's there.

28
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:13,681
[SPEAKER_01]: You can almost see where you're going in your life.

29
00:02:14,502 --> 00:02:14,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely.

30
00:02:14,942 --> 00:02:15,703
[SPEAKER_00]: You go to seclusion.

31
00:02:15,923 --> 00:02:17,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Or what you're running away from in your life.

32
00:02:17,924 --> 00:02:20,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Or what responsibility you're trying to avoid in your life.

33
00:02:21,246 --> 00:02:24,248
[SPEAKER_00]: So, amplification is good, but not in itself.

34
00:02:25,188 --> 00:02:28,030
[SPEAKER_00]: It's good if used rightly, if used skillfully.

35
00:02:29,499 --> 00:02:33,161
[SPEAKER_01]: To highlight the symptoms that you're trying to discern.

36
00:02:35,203 --> 00:02:37,644
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, solitude, yeah, it's necessary.

37
00:02:38,585 --> 00:02:46,250
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in itself, solitude does not result in wisdom or any development if you don't make the effort to develop and undo unwholesome states.

38
00:02:49,191 --> 00:02:50,512
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's good to challenge yourself.

39
00:02:51,253 --> 00:02:52,313
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely, yeah.

40
00:02:52,654 --> 00:02:53,534
[SPEAKER_00]: And go try it.

41
00:02:53,594 --> 00:02:56,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you don't challenge your situation,

42
00:02:58,622 --> 00:03:00,183
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not gonna see what's underlying it.

43
00:03:01,644 --> 00:03:06,668
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the... People think challenging situation means exposing yourself to sensuality and temptations.

44
00:03:06,749 --> 00:03:06,929
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

45
00:03:07,689 --> 00:03:11,272
[SPEAKER_00]: That's basically proliferating your ignorance.

46
00:03:11,352 --> 00:03:14,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Challenging situation is practicing withdrawal, bit by bit.

47
00:03:15,596 --> 00:03:20,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Seclusion, boredom, anxiety of solitude and loneliness.

48
00:03:21,460 --> 00:03:22,641
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's how you start challenging.

49
00:03:23,322 --> 00:03:26,805
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, the fundamental structure of your experience, not just the particular issue.

50
00:03:27,445 --> 00:03:29,387
[SPEAKER_00]: That you might be bothered or you're trying to overcome.

51
00:03:31,188 --> 00:03:32,269
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to amplify it.

52
00:03:32,509 --> 00:03:35,712
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to put put a magnifying glass and then see what shows.

53
00:03:35,812 --> 00:03:37,693
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you know exactly where your work is.

54
00:03:38,494 --> 00:03:41,396
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, you use solitude rightly, even with the wrong view.

55
00:03:43,618 --> 00:03:51,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Even without, you know, having developed right view and jhānas and samādhi and so on, you can still use solitude to your advantage.

56
00:03:51,204 --> 00:03:52,585
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's obviously, it's going to be unpleasant.

57
00:03:53,826 --> 00:03:54,727
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the whole point.

58
00:03:56,502 --> 00:04:00,363
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if your mind is most of the time lustful, most of the time angry,

59
00:04:25,872 --> 00:04:28,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Most of the time, distracted, you go into seclusion.

60
00:04:29,054 --> 00:04:29,915
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what's going to be amplified.

61
00:04:29,935 --> 00:04:31,817
[SPEAKER_01]: For a few days, it might not be those things.

62
00:04:32,277 --> 00:04:33,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Quite novelty experience.

63
00:04:33,638 --> 00:04:34,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure, sure.

64
00:04:34,118 --> 00:04:34,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Beautiful.

65
00:04:34,879 --> 00:04:35,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Give it some time.

66
00:04:35,439 --> 00:04:35,920
[SPEAKER_01]: The forest is open.

67
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:36,640
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not instant.

68
00:04:36,920 --> 00:04:39,562
[SPEAKER_00]: The moment you close the door, you're alone, everything gets amplified.

69
00:04:39,602 --> 00:04:40,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, sometimes.

70
00:04:41,264 --> 00:04:43,185
[SPEAKER_00]: But, but no, no, that's it.

71
00:04:43,385 --> 00:04:43,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

72
00:04:43,866 --> 00:04:47,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Settle in seclusion and then you'll see what starts bubbling up from the bottom.

73
00:04:49,190 --> 00:04:50,170
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's another thing.

74
00:04:50,210 --> 00:04:52,172
[SPEAKER_00]: See, if you, if you never amplify,

75
00:04:53,753 --> 00:04:56,875
[SPEAKER_00]: Even unwholesome states, you'll never realize how serious they are.

76
00:04:58,476 --> 00:05:02,879
[SPEAKER_00]: How, how, how deeply rooted those issues are.

77
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:09,663
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people, by default, people who are not interested in practice, that's why nobody likes solitude.

78
00:05:10,464 --> 00:05:16,288
[SPEAKER_00]: Everybody tries to find constantly something to keep their mind occupied, something to keep them flowing on the surface.

79
00:05:17,669 --> 00:05:22,592
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, fundamentally, what solitude will amplify is the unpleasant state of existence.

80
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:26,482
[SPEAKER_00]: unpleasant recognition.

81
00:05:26,722 --> 00:05:39,710
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't necessarily fully grasp it or fully admit it, for that matter, but you certainly start getting hints of that fundamental lack of control in regard to your feelings, in regard to your existence, in regard to your life, in regard to everything you hold dear to you.

82
00:05:41,091 --> 00:05:47,155
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why solitude often results in... well, first boredom, then that boredom becomes existential dread.

83
00:05:57,821 --> 00:05:58,301
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha...

84
00:05:58,321 --> 00:06:12,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha encourages people, or monks, not to go into seclusion, maybe for long periods, unless they've got a grasp on their situation, on their minds, they can handle things.

85
00:06:12,466 --> 00:06:21,630
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean that you should spend most of your day occupied with others, or hanging out with others, or just being physically present with others.

86
00:06:21,850 --> 00:06:22,010
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

87
00:06:22,390 --> 00:06:24,291
[SPEAKER_00]: You shouldn't go to the complete wilderness.

88
00:06:24,885 --> 00:06:27,366
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going to be fearing for your life and whatnot.

89
00:06:27,526 --> 00:06:31,727
[SPEAKER_00]: After utmost wild seclusion, it will be too much to handle.

90
00:06:32,247 --> 00:06:36,668
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not to say, oh, you should socialize because of that.

91
00:06:36,808 --> 00:06:36,988
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

92
00:06:37,108 --> 00:06:37,688
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

93
00:06:39,948 --> 00:06:46,870
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you prepare yourself for that kind of seclusion, with lesser amounts of seclusion, you'll never prepare yourself for that.

94
00:06:48,058 --> 00:06:58,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the problem, as I said before, where people try to determine the environment and the circumstances, whether that will do the practice for you.

95
00:06:58,902 --> 00:07:00,583
[SPEAKER_00]: So, is seclusion good or is seclusion bad?

96
00:07:01,763 --> 00:07:02,804
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends why you do it.

97
00:07:03,744 --> 00:07:04,745
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends what you amplify.

98
00:07:04,765 --> 00:07:08,466
[SPEAKER_00]: It depends whether you're ignoring what you're amplifying.

99
00:07:08,506 --> 00:07:13,228
[SPEAKER_00]: But if people don't see that, then they'll either blame seclusion or praise seclusion for wrong reasons.

100
00:07:14,585 --> 00:07:22,210
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I went in too much seclusion, too much soon, my mind couldn't handle it, so I develop a view, seclusion is bad.

101
00:07:23,371 --> 00:07:24,592
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, I don't do it.

102
00:07:26,533 --> 00:07:28,555
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's absolutely no reason to develop that view.

103
00:07:28,735 --> 00:07:31,917
[SPEAKER_00]: That was you overdoing it, based on a bad experience.

104
00:07:33,533 --> 00:07:36,174
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start discerning and realize, well, it's just an amplifier.

105
00:07:36,414 --> 00:07:38,634
[SPEAKER_00]: It amplified my bad states of mind.

106
00:07:38,755 --> 00:07:42,976
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what if I, as the Buddha would say in the suttas, a wise man would reflect thus.

107
00:07:43,376 --> 00:07:53,139
[SPEAKER_00]: What if I were to undo my unwholesome states, or at least suppress them to some degree, through my mindfulness and discernment and effort and virtue, and then I try seclusion?

108
00:07:54,339 --> 00:07:57,900
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha said, there would be absolute bliss and pleasure.

109
00:07:57,920 --> 00:07:59,580
[SPEAKER_00]: The pleasure of seclusion.

110
00:07:59,600 --> 00:08:01,261
[SPEAKER_00]: The joy that nothing else can match.

111
00:08:02,355 --> 00:08:11,641
[SPEAKER_00]: But it needs to be developed in that manner.

112
00:08:11,661 --> 00:08:23,688
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's so that people leave themselves out of the equation while judging if seclusion... No.

113
00:08:24,048 --> 00:08:25,209
[SPEAKER_01]: The seclusion is bad.

114
00:08:25,489 --> 00:08:25,689
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

115
00:08:25,989 --> 00:08:27,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's bad because of you.

116
00:08:28,511 --> 00:08:28,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

117
00:08:28,851 --> 00:08:30,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's what I mean.

118
00:08:30,272 --> 00:08:32,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, there's a natural tendency in everyone.

119
00:08:33,175 --> 00:08:38,980
[SPEAKER_00]: To develop like a factual view that pertains to factual, seemingly factual state of things.

120
00:08:39,861 --> 00:08:41,743
[SPEAKER_00]: As a universal independent of me.

121
00:08:41,883 --> 00:08:42,503
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a fact.

122
00:08:43,064 --> 00:08:44,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Has nothing to do with me.

123
00:08:45,666 --> 00:08:53,553
[SPEAKER_00]: But when it comes to practice of dhamma, most of the things would be determined by where your intentions are rooted.

124
00:08:53,873 --> 00:08:55,134
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what makes it good or bad.

125
00:08:55,274 --> 00:08:57,056
[SPEAKER_00]: Not universal rule.

126
00:08:58,353 --> 00:09:04,317
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you can, you know, you can go on a meditation retreat for a completely, utterly, unwholesome, wrong reasons.

127
00:09:05,138 --> 00:09:08,300
[SPEAKER_00]: But on paper, meditation is always good.

128
00:09:09,421 --> 00:09:12,663
[SPEAKER_00]: But you're doing it to avoid your solace, to avoid practice, to avoid responsibility.

129
00:09:15,726 --> 00:09:17,367
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what I mean.

130
00:09:17,807 --> 00:09:21,430
[SPEAKER_00]: There are obvious things that are always bad, and that's where the precepts come in.

131
00:09:21,610 --> 00:09:22,911
[SPEAKER_00]: You just never do those things.

132
00:09:23,727 --> 00:09:30,633
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, everything else that's more refined than that, will be determined by your intentions.

133
00:09:31,413 --> 00:09:36,458
[SPEAKER_00]: As I gave the example before, multiple times, like, oh, I want to go to the library and read the suttas.

134
00:09:37,178 --> 00:09:41,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, if you say that like that, it's a good thing, isn't it?

135
00:09:41,842 --> 00:09:42,243
[SPEAKER_00]: It's great.

136
00:09:43,203 --> 00:09:45,806
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you, like, start looking at it, why you want to do it?

137
00:09:46,406 --> 00:09:50,910
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because there's, like, lots of people there, and you're going to end up socializing, maybe even meet like-minded people.

138
00:09:51,839 --> 00:09:54,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, you don't necessarily explicitly think about it.

139
00:09:54,881 --> 00:09:57,823
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the nature of inauthentic attitude and bad faith.

140
00:09:57,883 --> 00:10:03,266
[SPEAKER_00]: You turn a blind eye to, for yourself, to yourself, to what the real reason is.

141
00:10:04,587 --> 00:10:10,471
[SPEAKER_00]: What the real reason is that I want to go to such and such place and, oh, read the suttas and do meditation.

142
00:10:10,551 --> 00:10:15,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's really because I feel a bit bored or a bit lonely or a bit scared or a bit this or a bit that.

143
00:10:15,414 --> 00:10:16,515
[SPEAKER_00]: I need distraction.

144
00:10:17,335 --> 00:10:18,596
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not always the reason.

145
00:10:19,216 --> 00:10:20,317
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when that is not the reason,

146
00:10:21,763 --> 00:10:30,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, if the reason is genuinely, I know, I want to find all these suttas, I spent three days thinking about it, that now means, okay, it's a good reason, good thing.

147
00:10:31,131 --> 00:10:40,519
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, like, the things that are unavoidable, like, things that you can do without, such as, as Buddha said, sexual intercourse or sensuality, you just, you do without.

148
00:10:40,999 --> 00:10:41,780
[SPEAKER_00]: You just stop doing it.

149
00:10:42,401 --> 00:10:45,063
[SPEAKER_00]: But things that you can't do without, such as eating food,

150
00:10:46,673 --> 00:10:47,696
[SPEAKER_00]: That will be determined.

151
00:10:47,716 --> 00:10:50,483
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not always unwholesome, even if it's the food you really like.

152
00:10:51,185 --> 00:10:55,134
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unwholesome if you eat it carelessly, which means if you eat it for the wrong reasons.

153
00:10:55,826 --> 00:11:03,468
[SPEAKER_00]: If you eat it for the reasons of carelessness, means automatically you've been eating it for the reasons of sensual enjoyment, not for the genuine, factual hunger.

154
00:11:04,368 --> 00:11:19,391
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's your responsibility, then, to reroute your reasons in the right foundation of hunger, sustenance of the body, basic health and strength, so that you can carry on a slightly harder lifestyle of solitude and so on, so that you can practice the dhamma.

155
00:11:21,131 --> 00:11:24,892
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you forget those reasons, then you automatically eat them for sensual reasons.

156
00:11:28,183 --> 00:11:30,865
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't look at the reasoning, then you'll be blaming the food.

157
00:11:32,047 --> 00:11:40,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you'll be trying to avoid the taste, or blaming the taste, or not eating, or fasting, or like giants, starve yourself to death, thinking that's the ultimate renunciation.

158
00:11:41,495 --> 00:11:42,536
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the point.

159
00:11:42,556 --> 00:11:49,682
[SPEAKER_00]: So, things like food, and there are many other things, they are not in themselves, neither wholesome nor unwholesome.

160
00:11:50,263 --> 00:11:51,104
[SPEAKER_00]: They're a necessity.

161
00:11:51,723 --> 00:11:55,344
[SPEAKER_00]: But, that doesn't mean that, oh, there's a necessity, I can eat it carelessly.

162
00:11:55,404 --> 00:11:55,524
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

163
00:11:55,704 --> 00:11:58,745
[SPEAKER_00]: By eating it carelessly, you make the necessity unwholesome.

164
00:12:00,726 --> 00:12:04,007
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the responsibility for wholesome and unwholesome lies where your intentions are.

165
00:12:04,087 --> 00:12:07,468
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why every intention, every action is intention.

166
00:12:07,948 --> 00:12:09,008
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in intention.

167
00:12:09,128 --> 00:12:14,090
[SPEAKER_00]: So, good action, bad action, neutral action will be so, based on good, bad or neutral intentions.

168
00:12:17,269 --> 00:12:24,933
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's easier to sort of develop a view and try to make it factual and just blame things or make things responsible for your intentions.

169
00:12:25,893 --> 00:12:27,694
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, no, I'm hungry, I must eat.

170
00:12:28,414 --> 00:12:29,375
[SPEAKER_00]: That's true on one level.

171
00:12:30,195 --> 00:12:36,659
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you eat carelessly, means your hunger will be secondary to your sensual enjoyment of that food.

172
00:12:38,099 --> 00:12:41,581
[SPEAKER_00]: If you eat carefully, means sensual enjoyment will be secondary.

173
00:12:42,427 --> 00:12:46,990
[SPEAKER_00]: And you become transparent by dwelling on those reasons, by not turning a blind eye.

174
00:12:47,010 --> 00:12:48,551
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point.

175
00:13:04,522 --> 00:13:08,723
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you're going to seclusion, for example, you have to have the right reasons beforehand?

176
00:13:08,803 --> 00:13:08,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

177
00:13:09,284 --> 00:13:10,444
[SPEAKER_00]: You clarify the reasons.

178
00:13:10,504 --> 00:13:14,085
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not sure whether they're right or wrong, well, go a little bit, and then see what comes up.

179
00:13:14,325 --> 00:13:15,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, why am I doing this?

180
00:13:15,506 --> 00:13:15,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

181
00:13:15,826 --> 00:13:20,487
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you can't handle seclusion, okay, you go back, but not like, oh, I'll never try this again.

182
00:13:20,847 --> 00:13:22,928
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't handle, not because seclusion is at fault.

183
00:13:23,608 --> 00:13:26,829
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't handle because my mind is at fault, because I'm not strong enough.

184
00:13:28,030 --> 00:13:31,591
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'll then practice and develop more strength, so that I can handle it.

185
00:13:32,892 --> 00:13:33,592
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not optional.

186
00:13:33,673 --> 00:13:34,733
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the thing.

187
00:13:34,853 --> 00:13:39,456
[SPEAKER_00]: Amplifying the foundations of your mind through seclusion is not optional.

188
00:13:41,758 --> 00:13:48,722
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, amplification can be bad as well, if you haven't done necessary work beforehand.

189
00:13:57,167 --> 00:14:00,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the state of your mind is more... is much more clearer.

190
00:14:01,222 --> 00:14:01,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

191
00:14:01,542 --> 00:14:02,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Because no one's around.

192
00:14:02,682 --> 00:14:04,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there's nothing taking away your attention.

193
00:14:05,343 --> 00:14:11,004
[SPEAKER_00]: And by not being distracted in that, like, basic manner, what you're going to stay with is the states of your mind.

194
00:14:11,104 --> 00:14:14,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, whether you want it or not, you're going to start seeing them more clearly.

195
00:14:15,825 --> 00:14:16,745
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what the Buddha said.

196
00:14:17,185 --> 00:14:23,007
[SPEAKER_00]: One cannot develop the sign of his own mind, cittanimitta, without having established himself in seclusion.

197
00:14:24,107 --> 00:14:28,868
[SPEAKER_00]: And without having developed the sign of the mind, cittanimitta, how can one develop the right view?

198
00:14:29,068 --> 00:14:29,728
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

199
00:14:31,189 --> 00:14:34,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, that's what I mean, like, amplification is not optional.

200
00:14:35,092 --> 00:14:38,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, my practice is so good I can do without solitude.

201
00:14:38,695 --> 00:14:38,835
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

202
00:14:39,415 --> 00:14:41,377
[SPEAKER_00]: Practice and solitude are almost synonym.

203
00:14:42,117 --> 00:14:44,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Practice is amplifying these things that need to be undone.

204
00:14:45,039 --> 00:14:48,262
[SPEAKER_00]: Amplifying wrong views because of which you get to abandon them.

205
00:14:59,519 --> 00:15:11,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you start getting angry in solitude, because of whatever thoughts you're having, you're getting angry, you can then see exactly what you do about that.

206
00:15:12,389 --> 00:15:12,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely.

207
00:15:12,829 --> 00:15:14,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Like what...

208
00:15:14,410 --> 00:15:15,511
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it shows where you're at.

209
00:15:15,852 --> 00:15:17,633
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I deal with anger?

210
00:15:18,854 --> 00:15:26,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, well, the boundaries, the lines you cross from your mental thought of anger to a verbal expression of anger to physical

211
00:15:27,375 --> 00:15:29,877
[SPEAKER_00]: Expressions of anger are much clearer in solitude.

212
00:15:30,517 --> 00:15:33,859
[SPEAKER_00]: You realize that actually there is no connection between the three.

213
00:15:33,919 --> 00:15:39,763
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that thought automatically seeps into speech, that then automatically seeps into physical action.

214
00:15:39,823 --> 00:15:39,963
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

215
00:15:40,583 --> 00:15:42,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Thought always remains within the domain of thought.

216
00:15:43,045 --> 00:15:45,707
[SPEAKER_00]: Speech always remains within the domain of speech.

217
00:15:45,767 --> 00:15:48,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Bodily action always remains within the domain of bodily action.

218
00:15:48,308 --> 00:15:51,530
[SPEAKER_00]: They are completely separate domains, unaware of each other.

219
00:15:51,690 --> 00:15:52,311
[SPEAKER_00]: You can say like that.

220
00:15:52,411 --> 00:15:55,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Three simultaneous domains of your three types of intentions.

221
00:15:56,762 --> 00:16:01,607
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's thought with ignorance goes for speech, goes for the physical action.

222
00:16:02,608 --> 00:16:05,971
[SPEAKER_00]: But you, your ignorance, is that bridge that connects it.

223
00:16:06,051 --> 00:16:06,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

224
00:16:07,733 --> 00:16:11,076
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you live in the environment that revolves around it, it's automatic.

225
00:16:11,336 --> 00:16:13,018
[SPEAKER_00]: I think bad, I speak bad.

226
00:16:13,798 --> 00:16:14,939
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm angry, I say it.

227
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:16,781
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just effortless.

228
00:16:16,961 --> 00:16:18,923
[SPEAKER_00]: It's with the grain, so to speak.

229
00:16:19,584 --> 00:16:20,505
[SPEAKER_00]: But in the solitude,

230
00:16:21,754 --> 00:16:32,216
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, that's kind of... you can benefit from... if you started practicing that restraint of bodily action, verbal action, then you get to see that mental action stays on the level of mind.

231
00:16:32,796 --> 00:16:36,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you ever thought, I'm gonna go and say something, then you go and say something.

232
00:16:36,417 --> 00:16:41,278
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying something and thinking something are two simultaneous things, present together, but not the same.

233
00:16:42,258 --> 00:16:46,319
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that the same thing channeled from thoughts into a speech and actions.

234
00:16:46,419 --> 00:16:46,559
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

235
00:16:46,959 --> 00:16:48,840
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your magical belief that connects them.

236
00:16:49,400 --> 00:16:50,300
[SPEAKER_00]: Your assumption.

237
00:16:51,569 --> 00:16:54,570
[SPEAKER_00]: It's pretty much your wrong view, your upādāna.

238
00:16:59,571 --> 00:17:11,535
[SPEAKER_00]: But in solitude, that boundary of you crossing it magically, my thought means my speech, means my action, becomes more apparent, and you lose your own belief in it.

239
00:17:12,235 --> 00:17:15,476
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people need to believe in their own anger in order to act angrily.

240
00:17:17,077 --> 00:17:18,318
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to make that leap.

241
00:17:19,319 --> 00:17:22,901
[SPEAKER_00]: Angry thoughts cannot make you punch somebody or say something bad.

242
00:17:23,862 --> 00:17:32,487
[SPEAKER_00]: It's your carelessness in regard to the angry thoughts that make you develop belief that punching or saying something offensive is how this is expressed.

243
00:17:33,668 --> 00:17:43,375
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the more you give in to anger, the more of the ways of expressing it proliferate, which means the easier it's going to be to express it in that magical belief manner.

244
00:17:45,496 --> 00:17:46,577
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you start restraining

245
00:17:48,082 --> 00:17:59,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Your belief of expressing the anger through your physical action, you realize, yeah, I'm angry, mentally, but what does that have to do with me harming this lump of flesh in this body?

246
00:17:59,566 --> 00:18:00,427
[SPEAKER_00]: It has nothing to do with it.

247
00:18:01,527 --> 00:18:03,228
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to believe that it has something to do with it.

248
00:18:04,008 --> 00:18:07,649
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why people, sometimes, when they get angry, they break inanimate things.

249
00:18:08,390 --> 00:18:16,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the belief of anger has been so proliferated, that, yeah, for them now, the things almost become like other people, that they can harm through their anger, in effect.

250
00:18:18,103 --> 00:18:18,684
[SPEAKER_00]: It's futile.

251
00:18:18,704 --> 00:18:22,366
[SPEAKER_00]: You know it's futile, but you can't help it, so you smash up thing that upsets you.

252
00:18:23,226 --> 00:18:24,968
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes that magical kind of attitude.

253
00:18:24,988 --> 00:18:27,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're trying to get the anger out.

254
00:18:27,629 --> 00:18:27,749
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

255
00:18:27,769 --> 00:18:31,191
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no other means of dealing with it, with the unpleasantness of anger.

256
00:18:31,612 --> 00:18:31,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

257
00:18:32,432 --> 00:18:37,475
[SPEAKER_00]: The only means were give in and try follow that way, and then that makes you follow it further and further and further.

258
00:18:37,695 --> 00:18:38,556
[SPEAKER_00]: But there is no end to that.

259
00:18:38,636 --> 00:18:39,156
[SPEAKER_00]: It's infinite.

260
00:18:39,476 --> 00:18:40,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Infinite proliferation.

261
00:18:41,875 --> 00:18:55,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one is overcome by anger, by... if you're shouting.

262
00:18:55,841 --> 00:18:56,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

263
00:18:56,862 --> 00:18:57,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what about sadness?

264
00:18:57,842 --> 00:19:01,484
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, in seclusion, there's just sadness, sad mind, sad...

265
00:19:01,504 --> 00:19:10,588
[SPEAKER_00]: If you do anything on account of any mental state, if you do anything by body or by your speech, means you couldn't... you could not have contained it.

266
00:19:11,182 --> 00:19:17,384
[SPEAKER_00]: You were too weak to contain it, so you gave in to the magical belief of expressing it through these other separate domains.

267
00:19:20,045 --> 00:19:34,491
[SPEAKER_00]: But once the clarity of that becomes apparent, that these domains are disconnected, in a way, you... you become unable to believe in expressing your anger or your sadness.

268
00:19:34,891 --> 00:19:36,792
[SPEAKER_00]: So you can't be angry, nor can you cry.

269
00:19:38,382 --> 00:19:41,863
[SPEAKER_00]: It just becomes disconnected.

270
00:19:44,963 --> 00:19:52,205
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha, when he was describing the nature of the speech, he said, having thought and pondered, having thought and pondered, one breaks into speech.

271
00:19:53,085 --> 00:19:58,166
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't say, having thought and pondered, then those thoughts and ponders, like, flow into the speech that comes out.

272
00:19:58,186 --> 00:19:59,486
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no connection.

273
00:19:59,526 --> 00:20:02,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Thinking and pondering, breaking into speech.

274
00:20:03,327 --> 00:20:04,207
[SPEAKER_00]: Two separate domains.

275
00:20:05,902 --> 00:20:07,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you must have thinking and pondering.

276
00:20:07,743 --> 00:20:12,006
[SPEAKER_00]: You know the domain of speech to be broken and spoken, but they don't connect.

277
00:20:12,206 --> 00:20:16,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly not in your assumed way of directionality of with the grain.

278
00:20:16,629 --> 00:20:18,690
[SPEAKER_00]: I think, I speak, I do.

279
00:20:18,790 --> 00:20:19,991
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be automatic.

280
00:20:20,111 --> 00:20:25,854
[SPEAKER_00]: What is uniting for puthujjana doing, speaking and thinking, is his sense of self, basically.

281
00:20:26,315 --> 00:20:31,078
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the more you undo the sense of self, the freer from action, three types of action you become.

282
00:20:33,399 --> 00:20:34,960
[SPEAKER_00]: Hence, the end of kamma.

283
00:20:36,503 --> 00:20:40,185
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you overcome with sadness, you see.

284
00:20:40,205 --> 00:20:41,826
[SPEAKER_00]: You're quite aware of that thought.

285
00:20:41,846 --> 00:20:46,248
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, so, if you try to get rid of it,

286
00:21:05,684 --> 00:21:07,525
[SPEAKER_01]: You think, I don't want the sadness.

287
00:21:07,545 --> 00:21:08,625
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm suffering on account of it.

288
00:21:08,645 --> 00:21:09,405
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want it.

289
00:21:11,146 --> 00:21:13,126
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, in a way, if you try to do anything on account of it.

290
00:21:14,867 --> 00:21:28,571
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if there is sadness and you've understood the nature of sadness, then you wouldn't have... well, you wouldn't even... you wouldn't even be able to consider

291
00:21:29,580 --> 00:21:34,642
[SPEAKER_00]: Giving in to the pressure and looking for the ways of doing something about it, because you're not affected by it in the first place.

292
00:21:34,662 --> 00:21:35,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Or expressing it.

293
00:21:35,882 --> 00:21:36,283
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

294
00:21:36,303 --> 00:21:38,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing something about it means expressing it.

295
00:21:38,323 --> 00:21:41,865
[SPEAKER_00]: People express it because that presents itself as a way of doing something about it.

296
00:21:42,585 --> 00:21:48,467
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not incidental that people have a view, or you must not repress your feelings because they'll find a way out.

297
00:21:48,927 --> 00:21:52,949
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you give in so badly at one point, because you've been trying to restrain yourself.

298
00:21:53,269 --> 00:21:53,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

299
00:21:53,969 --> 00:21:58,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure, you will amplify the pressure of giving in, but that's the whole point.

300
00:22:04,533 --> 00:22:09,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your knowledge should be the thing that cuts everything off.

301
00:22:09,218 --> 00:22:15,544
[SPEAKER_00]: The knowledge, the knowledge, the full understanding of the arisen phenomena, whatever that phenomena may be, sadness, anger, whatever,

302
00:22:16,753 --> 00:22:19,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Makes you not affected by it in the first place.

303
00:22:19,194 --> 00:22:22,256
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the true laying down the burden.

304
00:22:22,276 --> 00:22:23,536
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no burden of duty.

305
00:22:23,556 --> 00:22:26,178
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no burden of, I must do this, I must do that, I must avoid it.

306
00:22:26,478 --> 00:22:27,798
[SPEAKER_00]: When it arises, I must go this way.

307
00:22:27,819 --> 00:22:28,979
[SPEAKER_00]: When it comes this way, I must go that way.

308
00:22:28,999 --> 00:22:29,659
[SPEAKER_00]: I must manage it.

309
00:22:30,320 --> 00:22:33,901
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that becomes redundant, because you're not affected by it in the first place.

310
00:22:34,141 --> 00:22:37,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're looking at it from... And that's a true freedom, a relief.

311
00:22:38,243 --> 00:22:40,064
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, oh, it doesn't touch me.

312
00:22:40,104 --> 00:22:41,925
[SPEAKER_00]: This, what used to touch me, doesn't touch me anymore.

313
00:22:42,425 --> 00:22:43,166
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to do anything

314
00:22:44,533 --> 00:22:45,474
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to watch my breath.

315
00:22:45,514 --> 00:22:49,698
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to do anything because I've fully understood it and I'm not affected by it, in the first place.

316
00:22:50,058 --> 00:22:51,419
[SPEAKER_00]: And I have no doubt about it either.

317
00:22:51,940 --> 00:22:55,043
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, am I affected by it?

318
00:22:55,063 --> 00:22:55,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I not?

319
00:22:56,024 --> 00:23:02,990
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, okay, that lack of clarity regarding whether you're affected by it or not, affects you.

320
00:23:03,771 --> 00:23:04,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're affected by it.

321
00:23:05,492 --> 00:23:11,655
[SPEAKER_00]: So, honestly, transparently, you admit, and then you go further in your discernment, in your reflection, until... Am I affected by it?

322
00:23:12,116 --> 00:23:12,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I?

323
00:23:12,396 --> 00:23:12,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, maybe.

324
00:23:13,016 --> 00:23:14,877
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if I were affected, would I suffer?

325
00:23:14,917 --> 00:23:15,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

326
00:23:15,257 --> 00:23:15,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I suffer?

327
00:23:16,038 --> 00:23:16,198
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

328
00:23:16,798 --> 00:23:21,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in this particular case, at least, I'm not affected, because there's no suffering, nor there is any pressure to do something about this.

329
00:23:22,621 --> 00:23:23,202
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just there.

330
00:23:23,682 --> 00:23:24,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Literally.

331
00:23:25,323 --> 00:23:25,563
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

332
00:23:25,803 --> 00:23:26,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I be affected?

333
00:23:26,624 --> 00:23:27,104
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not sure.

334
00:23:27,164 --> 00:23:28,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, let's keep a close eye on it and see.

335
00:23:29,724 --> 00:23:30,304
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then it comes up.

336
00:23:31,084 --> 00:23:33,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, maybe, maybe, but a bit less, a bit more.

337
00:23:33,425 --> 00:23:34,745
[SPEAKER_00]: And you keep reflecting.

338
00:23:34,865 --> 00:23:38,246
[SPEAKER_00]: You keep not ignoring that that's where the problem is.

339
00:23:38,766 --> 00:23:46,628
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is not that you don't know how to deal with it, or you haven't dealt with it quickly enough, or you haven't dealt with it properly enough, or skillfully.

340
00:23:46,648 --> 00:23:48,889
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is that you need to deal with it in the first place.

341
00:23:49,569 --> 00:23:52,309
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to find a place where you don't have to deal with anything.

342
00:23:53,490 --> 00:23:56,530
[SPEAKER_00]: But that place is not physically found on top of a mountain or something.

343
00:23:57,291 --> 00:23:59,111
[SPEAKER_00]: That place can only be found internally.

344
00:23:59,674 --> 00:24:09,859
[SPEAKER_00]: With nothing, with no internally arising feelings, none of them can touch you, none of them can affect you, without you needing to deny them, without you needing to indulge them, without you needing to ignore them.

345
00:24:10,639 --> 00:24:12,460
[SPEAKER_00]: Means feeling is there, fully arisen.

346
00:24:12,480 --> 00:24:13,320
[SPEAKER_00]: What if it arises more?

347
00:24:13,641 --> 00:24:14,201
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

348
00:24:14,581 --> 00:24:20,284
[SPEAKER_00]: Can arise to the infinite amount, no domain of feeling can affect you.

349
00:24:20,304 --> 00:24:22,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus, doesn't matter the intensity.

350
00:24:22,525 --> 00:24:26,927
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't worry about intensity, because you know you're free from the domain where this intensity is manifest.

351
00:24:28,507 --> 00:24:47,840
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether it's, like, I don't know, something insignificant, like a... like a... like a... whether it's something insignificant, like a bad day, and you couldn't do things you wanted, or whether it's your dying parent in front of you, doesn't matter the intensity of this pleasure, you're not affected by it, because you abandoned the domain of it.

352
00:24:48,421 --> 00:24:50,482
[SPEAKER_00]: You understood the domain of it, which means abandon it.

353
00:24:50,502 --> 00:24:50,662
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

354
00:24:50,722 --> 00:24:55,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, everything then has to arise through your view, through the lens of

355
00:24:57,609 --> 00:24:59,190
[SPEAKER_01]: What is right?

356
00:25:01,631 --> 00:25:03,272
[SPEAKER_01]: You see everything through the right lens.

357
00:25:03,392 --> 00:25:03,912
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

358
00:25:03,932 --> 00:25:08,154
[SPEAKER_00]: That is the most fundamental, the widest point where things manifest.

359
00:25:08,614 --> 00:25:10,795
[SPEAKER_00]: So, from there, they're just going to be more and more particular.

360
00:25:11,115 --> 00:25:12,856
[SPEAKER_00]: But the widest point has been understood.

361
00:25:12,996 --> 00:25:17,399
[SPEAKER_00]: The basis of feeling as pleasant, unpleasant, neutral.

362
00:25:17,699 --> 00:25:19,720
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're not concerned with the content.

363
00:25:19,740 --> 00:25:21,200
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, this is pleasant, this is unpleasant.

364
00:25:21,561 --> 00:25:24,482
[SPEAKER_00]: You're concerned with the fact that it's being felt in the first place.

365
00:25:24,942 --> 00:25:25,863
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what Sariputra meant.

366
00:25:25,903 --> 00:25:26,723
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever's felt,

367
00:25:27,083 --> 00:25:28,204
[SPEAKER_00]: It counts as suffering.

368
00:25:28,224 --> 00:25:29,045
[SPEAKER_00]: It counts as a problem.

369
00:25:29,065 --> 00:25:32,267
[SPEAKER_00]: It counts as something that needs to be undone and overcome.

370
00:25:32,607 --> 00:25:33,948
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what it's felt.

371
00:25:36,951 --> 00:25:40,794
[SPEAKER_01]: But with the right view, whatever is experienced... Yeah.

372
00:25:40,834 --> 00:25:41,955
[SPEAKER_00]: The right view means that.

373
00:25:42,115 --> 00:25:44,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Overcoming whatever's felt.

374
00:25:44,257 --> 00:25:47,459
[SPEAKER_00]: That means that whatever is felt in the content cannot affect you either.

375
00:25:48,860 --> 00:25:51,042
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're not affected by the feeling in itself.

376
00:25:52,123 --> 00:25:55,806
[SPEAKER_00]: Let alone pleasant, unpleasant or neutral manifestation of that feeling.

377
00:25:58,162 --> 00:26:08,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you're in seclusion, you can also start to see lust arising from a great, great distance.

378
00:26:08,289 --> 00:26:08,609
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

379
00:26:08,829 --> 00:26:10,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Or hatred arising from a great distance.

380
00:26:10,510 --> 00:26:12,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Even before it crosses the horizon, yeah.

381
00:26:13,032 --> 00:26:15,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Where before it would sneak up on you and just take over.

382
00:26:15,313 --> 00:26:15,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

383
00:26:15,994 --> 00:26:18,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you can see it just... Yeah.

384
00:26:18,555 --> 00:26:19,176
[SPEAKER_00]: The sign of it.

385
00:26:19,216 --> 00:26:22,578
[SPEAKER_00]: Hate or lust or anything wholesome, they always have to come the same way.

386
00:26:24,917 --> 00:26:26,058
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way...

387
00:26:26,118 --> 00:26:29,220
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that means that the only way they can come, is if you ignore them.

388
00:26:30,121 --> 00:26:32,703
[SPEAKER_00]: If you ignore it, it always comes this way.

389
00:26:32,903 --> 00:26:34,564
[SPEAKER_00]: It always manifests itself from distance.

390
00:26:34,604 --> 00:26:36,386
[SPEAKER_00]: It's getting closer and closer and closer.

391
00:26:36,966 --> 00:26:39,608
[SPEAKER_00]: It will get too close to you, when you can't do anything about it.

392
00:26:39,648 --> 00:26:40,769
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you've been looking away.

393
00:26:42,090 --> 00:26:44,412
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why distraction, i.e.

394
00:26:44,552 --> 00:26:50,957
[SPEAKER_00]: delusion, is pretty much the basis of proliferation for lust and ill-will.

395
00:26:51,417 --> 00:26:52,698
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's the most blamed word.

396
00:26:53,503 --> 00:26:59,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you haven't been ignoring it, means you cannot come that close, because it requires you to not look at it, in order to come to you.

397
00:27:00,127 --> 00:27:08,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the... if you see distraction, you can see the... understand distraction, you can see what it's connected to, what it's attached to, and down the line... Yeah.

398
00:27:09,192 --> 00:27:10,653
[SPEAKER_00]: You see what else comes through it.

399
00:27:10,713 --> 00:27:10,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

400
00:27:11,493 --> 00:27:12,574
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see all those things.

401
00:27:13,014 --> 00:27:13,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

402
00:27:13,515 --> 00:27:13,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

403
00:27:14,255 --> 00:27:14,595
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

404
00:27:15,196 --> 00:27:17,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why mindfulness you can't have too much of.

405
00:27:18,818 --> 00:27:20,699
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's... it's... it's always to be practiced.

406
00:27:21,908 --> 00:27:23,409
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't overdo mindfulness.

407
00:27:24,030 --> 00:27:25,631
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't overdo not ignoring.

408
00:27:26,892 --> 00:27:36,038
[SPEAKER_00]: You can overdo active thinking about this, or you can overdo non-active thinking about this, but you can't overdo just that awareness and recognition of what's present.

409
00:27:36,278 --> 00:27:37,099
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't undo that.

410
00:27:37,759 --> 00:27:38,580
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't overdo that.

411
00:27:42,225 --> 00:27:46,167
[SPEAKER_01]: You can start to sense what's connected with unwholesome, what's connected with wholesome.

412
00:27:46,187 --> 00:27:50,189
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes, that's what it means when we say picking up the tokens and the signs, the peripheral.

413
00:27:50,249 --> 00:27:53,190
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the indicators of this?

414
00:27:53,250 --> 00:27:55,511
[SPEAKER_00]: The connections become more apparent.

415
00:27:55,531 --> 00:27:56,012
[SPEAKER_01]: More obvious.

416
00:27:56,132 --> 00:27:56,332
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

417
00:27:57,112 --> 00:28:02,295
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, when the horizon changes like this, I end up doing such and such things out of sensuality.

418
00:28:02,895 --> 00:28:05,036
[SPEAKER_00]: And now you see it, because you've seen the horizon.

419
00:28:05,176 --> 00:28:08,638
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're ignoring it, then you just find yourself doing these unwholesome things.

420
00:28:09,930 --> 00:28:13,032
[SPEAKER_00]: But not because they're inevitably to be done.

421
00:28:13,132 --> 00:28:27,579
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, even if it's a thought of sensuality manifested on a... on a mental level, you are the one with your own sensual assumption that then crosses into verbal and physical domain.

422
00:28:28,800 --> 00:28:31,041
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no natural flow between these domains.

423
00:28:31,441 --> 00:28:33,802
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you are always responsible for your actions.

424
00:28:34,963 --> 00:28:37,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Because action requires crossing from that domain.

425
00:28:37,604 --> 00:28:39,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Thoughts of sensuality are not your action.

426
00:28:43,228 --> 00:28:44,209
[SPEAKER_00]: They manifest themselves.

427
00:28:45,111 --> 00:28:51,560
[SPEAKER_00]: But you entertaining, indulging, welcoming, adding more to it, acting physically, verbally out of it, that's your action.

428
00:28:52,061 --> 00:28:52,622
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why...

429
00:28:53,680 --> 00:28:58,764
[SPEAKER_00]: No amount of external circumstances will ever be responsible for you choosing to act a certain way.

430
00:28:59,264 --> 00:29:12,553
[SPEAKER_00]: Because in the most fundamental sense, you, yourself and nobody else is responsible for that belief that travels from the domain of thought, to the domain of speech, to the domain of physical action.

431
00:29:12,953 --> 00:29:15,475
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no connection between them, only your belief.

432
00:29:15,675 --> 00:29:16,536
[SPEAKER_01]: This is who I am.

433
00:29:17,016 --> 00:29:22,940
[SPEAKER_00]: So you could say, having thought upon that one breaks into speech, one breaks into action, because of his own wrong views.

434
00:29:24,217 --> 00:29:28,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of his own views affected by greed, aversion and ignorance.

435
00:29:32,803 --> 00:29:38,887
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why, wherever, whatever you do, you remain bound as the owner of that action, as the Buddha would say.

436
00:29:39,487 --> 00:29:44,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you are the bridge between thought and speech and the physical acts.

437
00:29:45,231 --> 00:29:53,717
[SPEAKER_00]: You remove that bridge, then literally you just have things manifested in physical domain, things manifested in mental domain, things manifested in verbal domain.

438
00:29:55,152 --> 00:30:12,218
[SPEAKER_01]: So, having an angry thought doesn't mean that automatically you're going to shout.

439
00:30:12,698 --> 00:30:13,738
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, exactly.

440
00:30:13,898 --> 00:30:17,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Or do something... You need to break into shouting.

441
00:30:17,339 --> 00:30:18,880
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to choose to do it.

442
00:30:19,000 --> 00:30:19,900
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to give in to it.

443
00:30:20,637 --> 00:30:22,398
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if it feels the most natural thing.

444
00:30:22,438 --> 00:30:27,400
[SPEAKER_00]: The reason why it feels the most natural, effortless thing is because you've never been practicing enough restraint about it.

445
00:30:28,560 --> 00:30:30,601
[SPEAKER_00]: So you've been smoothing the way, basically.

446
00:30:30,621 --> 00:30:33,902
[SPEAKER_00]: You've been greasing the groove of that particular expression.

447
00:30:35,783 --> 00:30:39,404
[SPEAKER_00]: But it didn't have to be greased that way, which means you cannot do it.

448
00:30:42,241 --> 00:30:48,364
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, if you keep chucking the water the same way down the hill, it's gonna make a little little trench all the way down eventually.

449
00:30:49,044 --> 00:30:51,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And the more you do it, the bigger the trench becomes.

450
00:30:51,185 --> 00:30:54,047
[SPEAKER_00]: In the end, you can just pour it gently, it will go down that trench.

451
00:30:54,087 --> 00:30:55,127
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't even have to throw it.

452
00:31:01,750 --> 00:31:08,774
[SPEAKER_00]: But, no amount of depth of the trench will make any difference if there is no water being poured down it in the first place.

453
00:31:10,310 --> 00:31:18,191
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not affected by angry thoughts, there will be no ways of looking for expression, whether established way of expressing the anger, or new ways of expressing the anger.

454
00:31:18,331 --> 00:31:33,234
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no anger, as in there is no need to express these arisen thoughts, means all these things, all these channels and trenches, they've been made through my careless behavior in the past, are dead, dried up, like a palm stump, cut at its root.

455
00:31:34,494 --> 00:31:36,955
[SPEAKER_00]: So you don't go and trim around the branches, or try to cover up

456
00:31:37,829 --> 00:31:40,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Level the trenches that your anger dug up.

457
00:31:40,552 --> 00:31:40,692
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

458
00:31:41,212 --> 00:31:43,874
[SPEAKER_00]: Just stop fueling, stop feeding it with the water.

459
00:31:44,435 --> 00:31:45,075
[SPEAKER_00]: It will dry up.

460
00:31:45,836 --> 00:31:47,438
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how many trenches stay.

461
00:31:47,878 --> 00:31:49,800
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is water running down them or not.

462
00:31:51,501 --> 00:31:54,103
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't go trim around the branches, you uproot it.

463
00:31:54,604 --> 00:31:56,626
[SPEAKER_00]: Then all the branches are dried up and they're done.

464
00:31:59,068 --> 00:32:03,732
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the nature of proliferation, branching out, creating trenches of expression and so on.

465
00:32:04,611 --> 00:32:07,812
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you trying to deal with that means you're not dealing with that which caused it.

466
00:32:07,912 --> 00:32:10,233
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not dealing with the problem, you're trying to address the symptoms.

467
00:32:11,074 --> 00:32:19,677
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is you not being able to overcome the need for expression of your action manifested on mental level, with your speech, with your body.

468
00:32:20,797 --> 00:32:25,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, no amount of sensual thoughts can result in you physically performing sensuality.

469
00:32:26,359 --> 00:32:32,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Only you can result in you physically performing sensuality, on account of being pressured by the sensual thoughts.

470
00:32:34,328 --> 00:32:36,808
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, no amount of pressure can automatically make you do it.

471
00:32:38,849 --> 00:32:45,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressure can make you decide to do it and give in to the magical belief that this is how sensuality can be expressed or satisfied.

472
00:32:46,210 --> 00:32:48,471
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you can never satisfy it, because it's separate domains.

473
00:32:49,211 --> 00:32:51,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Thought and desire, it's on one domain.

474
00:32:51,411 --> 00:32:54,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Your physical satisfaction, scratching the itch, is completely separate domain.

475
00:32:55,872 --> 00:32:59,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're the one... That's why sensuality is upādāna.

476
00:32:59,473 --> 00:33:00,213
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an assumption.

477
00:33:02,340 --> 00:33:04,721
[SPEAKER_00]: Taking it to be that which it isn't.

478
00:33:04,761 --> 00:33:07,743
[SPEAKER_00]: You take physical domain to be that expression of the mental domain.

479
00:33:08,103 --> 00:33:10,844
[SPEAKER_00]: But they're two separate domains, as I said, unaware of each other.

480
00:33:12,505 --> 00:33:13,446
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing to do with each other.

481
00:33:13,526 --> 00:33:15,126
[SPEAKER_00]: It's thought and physical action.

482
00:33:15,387 --> 00:33:18,348
[SPEAKER_00]: You are the one who makes them connect, so to speak.

483
00:33:19,489 --> 00:33:22,970
[SPEAKER_00]: They can't really connect, that's why freedom from sensuality is possible in the first place.

484
00:33:23,651 --> 00:33:28,793
[SPEAKER_00]: If they were truly connectable, means, yeah, you could truly satisfy your desires, they manifest on the level of your thinking.

485
00:33:29,454 --> 00:33:30,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Things could be released, you know.

486
00:33:30,714 --> 00:33:31,735
[SPEAKER_00]: But it requires belief.

487
00:33:32,590 --> 00:33:34,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Upādāna is a form of belief.

488
00:33:36,074 --> 00:33:43,241
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why belief in virtue and duty, self-view, like a belief, believing that there is an independent self, belief of sensuality.

489
00:33:44,142 --> 00:33:48,206
[SPEAKER_00]: You believe that that's what sensuality is, in that physical domain.

490
00:33:49,868 --> 00:33:52,611
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's your thought and desire, that's what sensuality is.

491
00:33:53,932 --> 00:33:54,473
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you stop

492
00:33:56,718 --> 00:34:06,988
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start restraining, which means you prevent the habit of that expression of it along the physical lines, in the physical domain, you will stop feeding that wrong belief.

493
00:34:08,009 --> 00:34:08,930
[SPEAKER_00]: And that will become apparent.

494
00:34:09,031 --> 00:34:10,152
[SPEAKER_00]: There is a thought of sensuality.

495
00:34:10,232 --> 00:34:13,935
[SPEAKER_00]: And in itself, that's already enclosed, that's already finished.

496
00:34:13,955 --> 00:34:15,177
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a phenomenon of desire.

497
00:34:17,130 --> 00:34:29,673
[SPEAKER_00]: But that, meaning, I now go and do this, and get... pursue this food, or this person, whatever I want, it's a completely separate thing, that only your magical belief united as one leading to the other.

498
00:34:30,793 --> 00:34:32,353
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it's irreversible as well.

499
00:34:32,493 --> 00:34:36,494
[SPEAKER_00]: Once that becomes apparent, you cannot then reintroduce that magical belief.

500
00:34:36,694 --> 00:34:42,116
[SPEAKER_00]: Only ignoring it makes you... enables you to maintain the connection between the two.

501
00:34:42,556 --> 00:34:43,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there isn't a connection.

502
00:34:45,720 --> 00:34:49,067
[SPEAKER_00]: So, ignoring that there is no connection, that's how you maintain the connection.

